WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.208
Hi, I'm Florin!

00:00:02.208 --> 00:00:05.878
People are running a giant model on cards built to mine crypto.

00:00:05.878 --> 00:00:07.713
The cards are the easy part.

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:13.416
This model's sparse attention only had kernels for the newest chips.

00:00:13.416 --> 00:00:16.232
A fork writes them for the older mining cards.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:21.529
A long prompt flows through the cheap linear layers first.

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:22.721
They keep no cache.

00:00:23.141 --> 00:00:24.838
Eleven layers run sparse attention.

00:00:24.838 --> 00:00:28.910
It keeps only the top keys, so a huge context stays cheap.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.973
Those kernels only existed for the newest chips.

00:00:31.973 --> 00:00:34.615
The fork writes them for the older architecture.

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:39.324
Now it runs across four modified mining cards, sixty-four gigabytes each.

00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:45.113
Split every layer across the cards, and one user gets the fastest answer.

00:00:45.113 --> 00:00:48.573
But the cards talk constantly, so it needs a wide link.

00:00:48.993 --> 00:00:52.856
Give each card a quarter of the layers, and they just pass activations.

00:00:52.856 --> 00:00:55.531
It reads prompts faster and fits the narrow bus.

00:00:57.233 --> 00:00:58.673
Why a plain port is slow here.

00:00:59.822 --> 00:01:01.963
Upstream sized blocks for sixty-four heads.

00:01:01.963 --> 00:01:06.601
Split four ways, each card has sixteen, so half the work was padding.

00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:10.494
Clamp a bad key to row zero and let the mask erase it.

00:01:10.494 --> 00:01:12.364
A slow gather becomes a fast load.

00:01:12.784 --> 00:01:14.839
About a third faster on long contexts.

00:01:14.839 --> 00:01:17.480
It loses on one short shape, and says so.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.184
A hundred sixty-two of a hundred sixty-four coding tasks, ninety-seven percent on math.

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:27.083
One person's self-run; I didn't re-run these.

00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:31.878
The model is not new and the cards are a shopping list.

00:01:31.878 --> 00:01:34.274
The method is the kernel someone wrote for Ampere.

00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:41.287
To recap: sparse attention with no kernel for these cards, a fork

00:01:41.287 --> 00:01:44.078
that writes one, and numbers that are one person's.

00:01:44.498 --> 00:01:46.564
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:47.154
I'm Florin.

00:01:47.154 --> 00:01:47.449
Bye!
